block='''// Reference names in git-trim
// ===========================
//
// Local branches live below `refs/heads/`.  A short local name is the
// part of the reference name below that prefix, so the local branch
// `master` is stored in the reference `refs/heads/master`.
//
// Remote-tracking branches live below `refs/remotes/`.  Their short
// names start with the name of the remote, followed by a slash and by
// the name of the branch on that remote.  For example, the branch
// `feature` on the remote `origin` is represented locally by the
// reference `refs/remotes/origin/feature`, and it corresponds to the
// branch `refs/heads/feature` in the remote repository itself.
//
// A remote also has a symbolic reference, `refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD`,
// which points at the remote-tracking branch for the remote's default
// branch.  Converting that target back to the corresponding branch in
// the remote repository therefore means replacing the
// `refs/remotes/<remote>/` prefix with `refs/heads/`.
// Other remote-tracking branches are converted in the same way,
// although they are handled closer to where they are enumerated.
//
// The distinction is important for several operations:
//
// * A local branch is deleted by its short name.
// * A branch in a remote repository is deleted by pushing the full
//   `refs/heads/...` name of that branch to the remote.
// * A remote-tracking branch is never deleted directly.  Deleting it
//   locally would only remove a cache of the remote state; the next
//   fetch would recreate it.
// * The upstream of a tracking local branch is a remote-tracking
//   branch, and an annotation for a diverged upstream names the branch
//   in the remote repository rather than the remote-tracking cache.
// * A symbolic remote HEAD whose target is not a remote-tracking
//   branch is malformed for git-trim's purposes.  The conversion below
//   therefore asserts that its input starts with `refs/remotes/`,
//   rather than silently producing a meaningless local reference.
//
// Worktree administrative files use the same reference names.  A
// worktree's `HEAD` file either contains a reference name, for a
// branch checked out in that worktree, or a commit identifier, for a
// detached checkout.  Only the former can protect a local branch from
// deletion, because only a branch checked out in another worktree is
// unavailable for deletion in this worktree.
//
// These helpers keep the conversions in one place so that the rest of
// the program does not have to repeat the prefix handling whenever it
// moves between local names, remote-tracking names, and the names of
// branches in remote repositories.
//
'''
assert block.count('\n')==66, block.count('\n')
s=s.replace('pub fn local_head_ref_from_remote_head_target', block+'pub fn local_head_ref_from_remote_head_target',1)
p.write_text(s)
print([i+1 for i,l in enumerate(s.splitlines()) if 'assert!' in l])
